Page Table Coherence between CPU/GPU (Denver CPU)
This is a home grown ARM64 CPU tightly coupled with GPU for HPC and provides CPU/GPU hw cache coherence but two separate MMUs. The goal of this project is to provide uniform address space between CPU/GPU.

My role is to scope out / drive Linux VM subsystem changes / hw changes required for unified address space implementation. In addition I am implementing asynchronous fault support for faults originating from GPU. This uses kernel worker threads that use the use_mm interface to switch in required user address space in kernel context and make the GPU fault look like a cpu fault. Other highlights include modifying PTE_DIRTY macros to lookup GPU PTEs in addition to CPU PTEs for LRU page eviction. This is to make sure pages accessed by GPU dont always get evicted by OS. set_pte_at and tlb_flush routines for ARM are also modified to do remote tlb shootdowns on the gpu. Working closely with hw to improve GPU TLB invalidate performance. To avoid routing every CPU TLB inval request to GPU borrowed an extra bit in ARM PTE to record if the PTE is being shadowed on the GPU. Profiling impact of CPU initiated GPU TLB invalidate on GPU threads and working with hw to decrease victim latency.


