2006 Sep ~ 2008 Dec (Fermi Thread Scheduling: Software Component Lead)
Lead the thread scheduling unit (Host) changes in Fermi from Software. Work involved closely interacting with OpenGL/CUDA/DirectX teams to identify hotspots, pitfalls in previous generations and drive changes with hw and implement required changes in Resource Manager.

Multiple PBDMA: In previous architectures there was a single unit (PBDMA) that would fetch instructions decode and feed to downstream engines. Based on perf experiments this was causing pipeline bubbles in the engine and not able to saturate PCIe bandwidth properly. My role here was to identify the perf problems and implement SW changes in Resource Manager to split/schedule instruction streams between multiple PBDMAs. Involved tweaking context switch timeslices based on workload characteristics.

Vista WDDM Scheduling Support in HW:  Vista OS scheduler queues work in a pipeline based on dependencies between user mode apps. In previous architectures we tried to mimic strict ordering of work on a time sliced scheduler by patching instruction stream with aquire/releases to force ordering. This caused severe bubbles and power wastage. 
Drove efforts to move this entirely in hw by maintaining separate work queues (runlists) fed directly by the OS. This is called Buffered Mode Scheduling. Implemented SW changes required to adapt to this new scheduling mode.

Perf Problems with Buffered Mode Scheduling: Even though work from different contexts was serialized in a queue, it wasnt taking advantage of prefetching or pipelining hence context load operations were becoming stalling. Drove efforts to fix this in hw by allowing hw to start work on context loading n-1 context while context n was being saved back. This allowed TLBs/caches/MMU directories to get properly warmed up before work start flowing through for the new context.

Per Engine Runlists: Runlists are SW queues containing schedulable contexts read directly by hw. Previous architectures used a single runlist, split this into separate nodes to avoid graphics and compute work tasks from accidentally serializing behind each other.

Redesigned runlist data structures to minimize locking with hw by using double buffering. Also moved to an indexed bitmap implementation to get better CPU caching and make searches faster.

Software methods(traps) allow for pipeline execution of CPU code with GPU code. Redesigned software method handling to be able to execute multiple methods from a single interrupt to avoid interrupt latencies. Also supports out of order execution of SW methods when they are interleaved with hw methods.

Self Modifying Code: To avoid PCIe latency work is regenerated from within the GPU or fetched and expanded by the GPU. This reqiures instruction pipeline patching on a live system. Worked closely with hw to provide SW support to do this with minimal noops and acquire release instructions. Helped interns write better debugger extensions to monitor instruction pipeline states.

Power Aware Scheduling (ELPG): When there is no incoming work for a certain engine a timer kicks in and we disable that engine and block scheduling any further work on it. Next time OS sends more work we take a trap revive the engine and let work flow through. My role here was to design safely stopping work scheduling and reviving scheduling taking into account multiple race conditions and without dropping instructions. The actual implementation was done by other members of my team.

Fault Recovery: Worked closely with hw team to design SW for recovery from page faults, engine hangs and other error conditions. Actual implementation was done by other members of my team.

Verification: I was responsible for driving SW verification of the Instruction Pipeline and Thread Scheduling units in hw. Designed wrote and drove several test suites including unit randoms for hw test bench, fullchip tests and large system stress tests.

